---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import { ProgressiveDisclosureVisualizer } from "../../components/ProgressiveDisclosureVisualizer";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Skills - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="requirements-writing" subChapterId="skills">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        Skills are reusable sets of instructions that an AI tool can load on
        demand. Think of them like a handbook for the AI: before starting a
        task, the AI skims the table of contents and pulls in only the
        instructions relevant to what you're asking it to do. This keeps the
        context window clean and focused, rather than stuffed with information
        that doesn't apply.
      </p>

      <h3>What Is a Skill?</h3>

      <p>
        At its core, a skill is a folder containing a <code>SKILL.md</code> file.
        This file includes metadata (a name and description, at minimum) and instructions
        that tell the AI how to perform a specific task. Skills can also bundle scripts,
        templates, and reference materials alongside the instructions.
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap"
      >
        {`my-skill/
  ├── SKILL.md      # Required: instructions + metadata
  ├── scripts/      # Optional: executable code
  ├── references/   # Optional: documentation
  └── assets/       # Optional: templates, resources`}
      </div>

      <p>
        The key difference between a skill and just dumping instructions into a
        prompt is that skills use <strong>progressive disclosure</strong>. The
        AI doesn't carry every skill in memory at all times. Here's how it
        works:
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-3 my-8">
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Discovery</strong> — At startup, the AI
            loads only the name and description of each available skill. Just enough
            to know when it might be relevant.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Activation</strong> — When your task matches
            a skill's description, the AI reads the full <code>SKILL.md</code> instructions
            into context.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3 items-start">
          <div
            class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-violet-100 text-violet-700 text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 mt-0.5"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-stone-800">Execution</strong> — The AI follows the
            instructions, optionally loading referenced files or running bundled scripts
            as needed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        This approach keeps the context window clean and focused, rather than
        stuffed with information that doesn't apply to the current task.
      </p>

      <ProgressiveDisclosureVisualizer client:visible />

      <Callout variant="success" title="Mental model">
        <p>
          Imagine handing a new team member a thick binder of every process your
          company has. They'd be overwhelmed and slow. Instead, you'd say "check
          the section on QA reviews before you submit your PR." Skills work the
          same way: the AI gets a table of contents, and only reads the chapter
          it needs right now.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Why Skills Matter</h3>

      <p>
        Without skills, you end up in one of two situations. Either you put all
        your instructions into a single large system prompt, or AGENTS.md file
        (which eats up context and slows down every interaction), or you
        manually paste relevant instructions into each conversation (which is
        tedious and easy to forget). Skills solve both problems by letting the
        AI self-serve the right instructions at the right time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Skills also make your AI workflows <strong
          >shareable and consistent</strong
        >. When a skill lives in a repository, every team member gets the same
        instructions. Updates to the skill propagate to everyone. New hires get
        the benefit of your team's accumulated best practices without needing to
        learn them all upfront.
      </p>

      <p>
        Skills started out as a Claude feature, but the format is now an open
        standard (originally from Anthropic) that many agents read: Claude Code,
        Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Gemini CLI, Codex, and VS Code among them. A
        skill you write for one tool works in the others, so you're not locked
        into a single vendor.
      </p>

      <h3>What Makes a Good Skill?</h3>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Focused scope
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            A skill should cover one specific task or workflow. "How to write
            acceptance criteria" is a good skill. "Everything about our project"
            is not.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Breaking down a task into smaller skills is a great way to get the
            AI to focus on the right things. And it will load the skills as
            needed. You can also link skills to each other to create more
            complex workflows.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Clear trigger
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            The skill should describe when it applies, so the AI knows when to
            load it. For example: "Use this skill when the user asks to create a
            Jira ticket" or "Use this skill when reviewing a pull request."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Concrete examples
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Include before/after examples or templates. AI tools follow examples
            much more reliably than abstract instructions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
            Kept up to date
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
            Treat skills like living documentation. If your process changes,
            update the skill. Outdated instructions lead to outdated output.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Getting Started</h3>

      <p>
        For a hands-on example of building a complete skill from scratch, check out
        the <a
          href="/competitive-research/structuring-your-output"
          >Structuring Your Output</a
        > page in the Competitive Research chapter. It walks through creating a
        competitive research skill with real instructions and examples.
      </p>

      <p>
        The company maintains a shared repository of skills that you can browse
        and use immediately:
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8"
      >
        <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2">
          Company Skills Repository
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Browse the collection of pre-built skills your team has put together.
          These cover common workflows and can be added to your projects
          directly.
        </p>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/konradgroup/ai-skills"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium"
        >
          github.com/konradgroup/ai-skills
        </a>
      </div>

      <p>
        To understand the Agent Skills format in more depth, check out <a
          href="https://agentskills.io/home"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >agentskills.io</a
        >, which documents the open standard. For pre-built skills to browse and
        install, the largest public directory is <a
          href="https://skills.sh"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >skills.sh</a
        >, Vercel's registry, installable with <code>npx skills add</code>. It's
        worth a look for inspiration, but read the security note below before
        installing anything from it.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="warning" title="Security note">
        <p>
          The public directories are large and barely curated. A 2026
          independent audit of skills across the ecosystem found roughly one in
          eight was outright malicious, and a lot of the rest is low-quality,
          AI-generated filler. Anyone can publish, and on some registries a
          skill shows up automatically from install telemetry with no review at
          all.
        </p>
        <p>
          So treat an external skill like any other untrusted code. <strong
            >Read it over before adding it to your project.</strong
          > Skills can contain arbitrary instructions and scripts. Rather than
          importing or cloning one directly, copy the parts you need after
          reviewing them. This is how you avoid a supply-chain attack where a
          malicious skill instructs the AI to do something harmful in your
          codebase.
        </p>
      </Callout>

    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
